as we celebrate the golden anniversary  of the protein data bank  let's look at the history and the  milestones that shaped it to become the  essential resource for research and  education  it is today the protein data bank was  established in 1971. in its first few  years the archive was very small  and contained a few groundbreaking  structures  for each structure the pdb contained the  three-dimensional coordinates  or the x y and z positions of each atom  how did the scientists see the atoms  back in the 1970s the sole method to do  so was x-ray crystallography  proteins form crystals when they are  purified and concentrated  within the crystal many copies of the  protein are arranged in symmetrical  arrays  when an x-ray beam strikes the crystal  the x-rays scatter into a distinctive  pattern of spots  these x-ray diffraction patterns are  then analyzed to determine the positions  of atoms in the protein  in 1913 the father-son team henry and  lawrence bragg were the first to  describe how to interpret the x-ray  diffraction pattern  [Music]  they used a sodium chloride or salt  crystal for their experiments  later the bragg's mentored many  world-class researchers who used x-ray  crystallography on a variety of  increasingly complex substances  [Music]  in 1958 using x-ray crystallography john  kendrew determined the first atomic  structure of a protein myoglobin  with that the field of structural  biology was born  more and more scientists started to  study the 3d structures of proteins and  by the early 1970s there were a dozen  atomic structures solved in different  labs  researchers around the world were  interested in exploring what these 3d  structures revealed  however data sharing was difficult in  the time before the internet  early pdb structures were stored on  paper punch cards  each containing information about one  atom  the protein data bank archive was  established to solve this problem  the scientists deposited the data and  the pdb copied it onto magnetic tapes  and distributed them to researchers upon  request  in october 1980 10 years after the pdb  was established the pdb  archive holdings contained 151  structures available for distribution  the following year the first dna  structure was added to the pdb  in 1989 the first structure determined  by a biophysical method called nuclear  magnetic resonance spectroscopy  or nmr was deposited to the pdb  in this method a purified protein is  mixed with special solvent and inserted  into an nmr probe  the protein sample is then exposed to a  strong magnetic field  this makes the nuclei of certain atoms  like hydrogen spin like tops  when the sample is probed with radio  waves the nuclei become excited and  resonate the frequencies are measured  and recorded  the surroundings of the atoms determine  how high or how low the frequencies are  using computational methods the  measurements are converted into a graph  that represents the frequencies as peaks  with specific locations for specific  atom groups  this information is further refined and  combined with additional nmr experiments  to determine the 3d structure  the nmr method proved to be effective in  solving 3d structures of smaller  proteins  and gave us a unique glimpse of how  proteins behave in solution  in 1991 the first structure determined  using 3d electron microscopy  or 3dem was released by the pdb  in this method a tiny amount of purified  protein is placed onto a copper grid  [Music]  a special machine spreads the sample in  a single layer on the grid  the sample is frozen in liquid ethane  and then loaded into an electron  microscope  the sample is exposed to a beam of  accelerated electrons and  images are captured  usually the proteins are in many  different orientations  the images are then grouped by  orientation and then combined  computationally to reconstruct the 3d  shape of the molecule  atoms are fit into the map to derive the  3d structure of the protein  3dem has become a perfect complement to  x-ray and nmr techniques  the method is very effective on large  complex assemblies that are typically  difficult to study by either of the two  older methods  in 1995 the pdb archive became  accessible for the first time through a  www website  the pdb holdings were accessible online  through various services since 1988.  however the new website equipped with a  browser marked a new chapter in data  distribution  researchers could still request the full  archive on cd-rom in 1999  the 10 000 structure was released into  the pdb  the distribution of the pdb archive on  physical media was discontinued in 2004.  by then the internet and computer  technology made it possible for all data  operations to be done  completely online through the ftp server  and web portals maintained by the  members of the newly established  worldwide pdb  organization in 2008 the pdb archive  reached another milestone number of  entries  50 000 structures in 2014 the number  doubled  as the pdb entered its golden year there  were more than 170 000 structures in the  archive  the growing numbers of 3d structures of  proteins and nucleic acids further our  understanding of biology and help us  explore how life works at the atomic  level  many nobel prizes have been awarded for  projects involving molecular structures  structural biology research is essential  in addressing major public health  emergencies  for example scientists have used the  structures of hiv proteins to design  drugs that stop the virus from  multiplying  today as the world faces the covid  pandemic  structural biologists are exploring its  viral proteins in a quest for new  therapeutics and vaccines  the pdb provides ready access to  structural biology research  ensuring the quality of the data and  providing tools for query  visualization and analysis of these  structures  now 50 years since the pdb's founding  the protein databank continues to be a  leading global resource for experimental  data that is playing a central role in  scientific discovery and education  [Music]  you
 

